  {
   "id": "BAAx8I9VZooJ",
   "from": "Ben Bond-Lamberty",
   "author": "f4249b01b99d0091",
   "date": "2013-07-29T13:27:02Z",
   "subject": "Re: Best plotting option with following data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Neither of these questions is really answerable by anyone except you--that is, we don&#39;t know what your purpose is here. Can you make *a* figure from this table? Sure. As before, start with a data frame:<div>\r\n<br></div><div>d &lt;-data.frame(community=c(&quot;Com1&quot;,&quot;Com1&quot;,&quot;Com2&quot;,&quot;Com2&quot;), village=c(&quot;Vil1&quot;,&quot;Vil2&quot;), gmean=c(290.05,322.54,13.67,38.14), gmedian=c(186,229,11,22))<br>\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>d_melt &lt;- melt(d,id.vars=c(1,2))<br></div><div><br></div><div>ggplot(d_melt,aes(village,value,fill=variable))+geom_bar(position=&quot;dodge&quot;,stat=&quot;identity&quot;)+facet_grid(community~.)<br>\r\n</div><div><br></div><div>...but how you plot it depends on what you want to show/emphasize of course.</div><div><br></div><div>Ben</div><div><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sun, Jul 28, 2013 at 12:50 PM, Carpo <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">sam.sc...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
