     3,
     "<br><br>On Monday, July 29, 2013 12:00:51 PM UTC-4, Dennis Murphy wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi:<div><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sun, Jul 28, 2013 at 9:50 AM, Carpo <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">sam.sc...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">Hello,<br><br>New to R and ggplot2, so pardon the naivete.<br>\n<br>I have the following table that someone has asked me to make a figure for (changed labels to make it generic):<br><br>\n\n<table style=\"width:401.5pt;margin-left:4.4pt;border-collapse:collapse\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" width=\"535\">\n <tbody><tr style=\"min-height:13pt\">\n  <td style=\"width:89.55pt;border-style:solid none;border-top-color:windowtext;border-top-width:1pt;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"119\">\n\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Community</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:92.2pt;border-style:solid none;border-top-color:windowtext;border-top-width:1pt;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"123\">\n\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Village</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:111.75pt;border-style:solid none;border-top-color:windowtext;border-top-width:1pt;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"149\">\n\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Mean\n  size of garden (m<sup>2</sup>)</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:1.5in;border-style:solid none;border-top-color:windowtext;border-top-width:1pt;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"144\">\n\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Median\n  size of gardens (m<sup>2</sup>)</span></p>\n  </td>\n </tr>\n <tr style=\"min-height:13pt\">\n  <td style=\"width:89.55pt;border:none;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"119\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Com1</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:92.2pt;border:none;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"123\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Vil1</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:111.75pt;border:none;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"149\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">290.05</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:1.5in;border:none;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"144\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">186</span></p>\n  </td>\n </tr>\n <tr style=\"min-height:13pt\">\n  <td style=\"width:89.55pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"119\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"> </span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:92.2pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"123\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Vil2</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:111.75pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"149\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">322.54</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:1.5in;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"144\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">229</span></p>\n  </td>\n </tr>\n <tr style=\"min-height:13pt\">\n  <td style=\"width:89.55pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"119\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Com2<br></span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:92.2pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"123\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Vil3</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:111.75pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"149\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">13.67</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:1.5in;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"144\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">11</span></p>\n  </td>\n </tr>\n <tr style=\"min-height:13pt\">\n  <td style=\"width:89.55pt;border-style:none none solid;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"119\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\"> </span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:92.2pt;border-style:none none solid;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"123\">\n  <p><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">Vil4</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:111.75pt;border-style:none none solid;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"149\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">38.14</span></p>\n  </td>\n  <td style=\"width:1.5in;border-style:none none solid;border-bottom-color:windowtext;border-bottom-width:1pt;padding:0in 5.4pt;min-height:13pt\" nowrap valign=\"bottom\" width=\"144\">\n  <p style=\"text-align:right\" align=\"right\"><span style=\"font-size:10pt\">22</span></p>\n  </td>\n </tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<br><br><br>I don&#39;t have the individual cases, however, so this is the only data to work off of. Questions: 1) what would be the best plotting option? and 2) is it even possible without the case values (I don&#39;t even have the stdev, for instance)? For the first question, I suspect a faceted or grouped boxplots may be the way to go. As for the second, I&#39;m not sure this is even possible.<br>\n<br></blockquote><div>For (i), a boxplot is impossible because you need at least a five-number summary of a distribution in order to produce one. Re (ii), your concern about a lack of a measure of variation is valid; it&#39;s generally not a great idea to compute statistical measures of location without some indication of the uncertainty in those measures. The only thing you could say with some certainty is that the distributions of interest are right-skewed since the means are always larger than the medians and the differences between them increase with magnitude (which would suggest that the corresponding measures of variation also increase with magnitude, but that is only speculation based on the limited information available).</div>\n<div><br></div><div>That said, here are a couple of ways you could plot your data. I would avoid bar charts in this case because they are intended to display discrete *distributions*, not numeric summaries thereof. There are better ways of displaying numeric summaries by group. The following is much less wasteful of ink.</div>\n<div><br></div><div><div>DF &lt;- data.frame(community = factor(rep(c(&quot;Com1&quot;, &quot;Com2&quot;), each = 2)),</div><div>                 village = factor(paste0(&quot;Vil&quot;, seq(4))),</div><div>                 Mean = c(290.05, 322.54, 13.67, 38.14),</div>\n<div>                 Median = c(186, 229, 11, 22))</div><div><br></div><div># &#39;stack&#39; the Mean and Median variables so that they can be used as</div><div># aesthetics in ggplot2. We use the very useful reshape2 package for this purpose.</div>\n<div>library(reshape2)</div><div><br></div><div># id.var indicates which variables are not to be stacked</div><div>DFm &lt;- melt(DF, id.var = c(&quot;community&quot;, &quot;village&quot;))</div><div>str(DFm)    # show the structure of the reshaped data</div>\n<div><br></div><div>library(ggplot2)</div><div><br></div><div>ggplot(DFm, aes(x = village, y = value, color = community, group = community)) +</div><div>    geom_point(size = 3) + geom_line(size = 1) +</div><div>    facet_wrap( ~ variable)</div>\n<div><br></div><div># The scales = &quot;free_x&quot; argument permits varying x-scales in each panel</div><div>ggplot(DFm, aes(x = village, y = value, color = variable, group = variable)) +</div><div>    geom_point(size = 3) + geom_line(size = 1) +</div>\n<div>    facet_wrap(~ community, scales = &quot;free_x&quot;)</div></div><div><br></div><div>Some other options:</div><div>   * plot the mean and median by village in a single panel;</div><div>   * create a Cleveland dot chart by village</div>\n<div><br></div><div><div>ggplot(DFm, aes(x = village, y = value, color = variable, group = variable)) +</div><div>    geom_point(size = 3) + geom_line(size = 1)</div><div><br></div><div>ggplot(DFm, aes(x = village, y = value, color = variable)) +</div>\n<div>    geom_point(size = 3) + geom_vline(aes(xintercept = 1:4), linetype = &quot;dotted&quot;) + </div><div>    coord_flip()</div></div><div><br></div><div>As a novice, it&#39;s useful for you to see various ways of visualizing the data in ggplot2. You can judge for yourself which one(s) you prefer, or try something completely different. There are multiple ways you could plot such data - which one is &#39;best&#39;, as Ben noted, is a function of the message you wish to convey to your target audience.</div>\n<div><br></div><div>Dennis</div><div><br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left-width:1px;border-left-color:rgb(204,204,204);border-left-style:solid;padding-left:1ex\">Thanks<span><font color=\"#888888\"><br>\n\n\n<p></p>\n\n-- <br>\n-- <br>\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the ggplot2 mailing list.<br>\nPlease provide a reproducible example: <a href=\"https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372404131000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2JbDVFELPAvti1zH4WtuMG\">https://github.com/hadley/devtools/wiki/Reproducibility</a><br>\n <br>\nTo post: email <a rel=\"nofollow\">ggp...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\nTo unsubscribe: email <a rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a><br>\nMore options: <a href=\"http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372404131000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0YJc5b5BIkdu2-DJAXcc4u\">http://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2</a><br>\n <br>\n--- <br>\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;ggplot2&quot; group.<br>\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a rel=\"nofollow\">ggplot2+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792372404131000&amp;usg=AOvVaw321kvL4GHThtoT7pUQxXJT\">https://groups.google.com/groups/opt_out</a>.<br>\n <br>\n <br>\n</font></span></blockquote></div><br></div></div>\n</blockquote>"
